# FounderOS — Rules for Human Developers & AI Coding Agents (Cursor, Antigravity, Claude)
#
# FounderOS is a deterministic agent kernel with a Telegram gateway that runs
# Turicks agency operations. The core is FROZEN: you extend it by adding tools
# and prompts at the declared extension points, never by rewiring the kernel.
# CI enforces most of this file (scripts/verify-architecture.ts). Violations
# fail the merge gate — do not attempt to route around them.

## 0. The one orchestration path (do not add another)

message → plan (LLM: PlannerDecision) → dispatch (PURE CODE supervisor)
        → worker (isolated TaskEnvelope, capped tools, ToolReceipts)
        → collect (Zod-validated StepResult) → … → synthesize → reply + receipts

- Every boundary is a Zod contract in `src/kernel/contracts.ts` (TaskEnvelope,
  Plan, StepResult, FailureReport, ToolReceipt). A contract mismatch is a
  terminal typed failure — never retry-and-hope, never loosen the schema.
- Action claims require successful ToolReceipts (`validateStepResult`). The
  synthesizer only ever sees validated results. Do not bypass this to "make
  the reply nicer" — it is the zero-hallucination mechanism.
- Routing, parsing, and guards are pure unit-tested functions. NEVER implement
  behavior as prompt instructions when a pure function can enforce it.

## 1. Where new code goes (extension points — the ONLY places you add features)

| You are adding…            | Put it in…                                        |
|----------------------------|---------------------------------------------------|
| A new external tool        | `src/tools/{name}.ts` implementing `UnifiedTool`  |
| Its LangChain wrapper      | `src/agents/agent-tools/{domain}.ts`              |
| Its department assignment  | `src/agents/capabilities.ts` (single source of truth) |
| A worker prompt change     | `src/agents/prompts/{department}.ts`              |
| A provider integration     | `src/infra/providers/` (ADR-029: tools call providers, never Composio/REST directly) |
| A DB table/query           | `src/db/schema.ts` + `src/db/queries/` + drizzle migration |
| A proof/report renderer    | `src/proof/`                                      |
| Tests                      | `tests/unit/**` mirroring the src path            |

New tool = the 8-point checklist in `docs/rules/TOOL-STANDARDS.md`. Short form:
1. `src/tools/{name}.ts` — pure `UnifiedTool`, no LangChain/LangGraph imports,
   returns `ToolResult { success, data?, error? }`. Soft-fail with an
   actionable `error`; never throw for expected provider failures.
2. `tests/unit/tools/{name}.test.ts` — mock the provider; test the failure
   paths (wrong field names and phantom successes are the historical bugs).
3. Wrapper in `src/agents/agent-tools/{domain}.ts`. Side-effecting tools get
   the HITL gate: DB row BEFORE interrupt(), side effects only after approval,
   idempotency-key check before every external send, audit row only on real
   success (`src/kernel/tool-adapter.ts` pins this ordering — do not reorder).
4. Register in `src/agents/capabilities.ts`. Capability text is GENERATED from
   this table — never hand-write "what can you do" prose.
5. `pnpm gate` green (lint + build + wiring + arch + tests).

## 2. Immutable core (read, do not modify without an ADR + founder approval)

- `src/kernel/*` — contracts, supervisor, graph, tool-adapter, state
- `src/gateway/kernel-boot.ts` — the ONLY composition root (models, tools,
  checkpointer are injected here; the kernel itself is a library)
- `src/infra/hitl.ts`, `src/infra/checkpointer.ts`
- `governance/architecture-baseline.json` — the debt ratchet; it may only
  shrink. Never hand-edit it upward.

## 3. Anti-slop invariants (CI fails your PR if you break these)

1. TOMBSTONES: killed v2 modules stay dead — office.ts, office-run,
   execution-guard, pre-router, fast-paths, domain subgraphs. Do not recreate
   them under any name.
2. Import direction: contracts ← kernel ← gateway. Kernel imports only
   kernel/core/db/infra/tools. Gateway never imports into kernel.
3. LOC budget: no src file over 400 lines. Split before you hit it.
4. Every fail-open catch needs `// allow-failopen: <reason>`.
5. No `any`, no `@ts-ignore`, no `console.log` (use `childLogger`). Fix the
   type, not the compiler.

## 4. Type-safety & style

- TypeScript strict. Zod-validate at every system boundary (user input,
  provider responses, DB reads feeding contracts).
- Immutability: return new objects, never mutate inputs.
- Temperature 0 everywhere; determinism is a feature, not a preference.
- Errors classify by HTTP status class (`src/agents/model.ts`):
  5xx/429/transport → retriable (jittered backoff in
  `src/gateway/model-retry.ts`); 404 → model fallback; 401/403 → fail LOUD.
- Never silently swallow errors. FailureReports name the real component.

## 5. Testing (the dev loop is $0 — this is non-negotiable)

- `pnpm test` uses scripted/mocked models only. A unit test that makes a real
  LLM/API call is a bug, full stop.
- Bug fixes start with a failing test (PR template section is mandatory).
- The full kernel graph runs offline: extend
  `tests/unit/kernel/kernel-e2e.test.ts` patterns for new flows.
- `pnpm eval` (live, paid) is a once-per-feature milestone gate, not a
  debugging loop. `pnpm qa:telegram` is production acceptance, run before
  release only.
- "Done" = the verification command run fresh with output shown (rule #24).
  Anything else is "NOT VERIFIED — <reason>". Say so explicitly.

## 6. Git & PR policy (CI-enforced by .github/workflows/branch-policy.yml)

- NEVER commit to `main`. NEVER open a PR targeting `main` (only the
  `beta → main` promotion PR, opened/merged by the founder).
- Flow: cut `cursor/*` or `feat/*` from a fresh `beta` → PR to `beta` →
  founder promotes `beta → main` → CD deploys.
- Every PR carries evidence: fresh `pnpm gate` output, and live-path proof or
  an explicit NOT VERIFIED with the reason.
- Conventional commits (`feat:`, `fix:`, `chore:`, …). Surgical diffs only —
  no drive-by refactors, no reformatting untouched lines.

## 7. Data & config discipline

- No hardcoded secrets, model names, or thresholds — env vars via
  `src/core/config.ts`; validate presence at boot (`src/infra/boot-validate.ts`).
- If a task fails on ambiguous requirements, fix the schema (planner asks for
  the missing field). Never guess data.
- Docs/ADR changes → `pnpm brain:sync` so the RAG brain stays truthful.
- Threads are never wiped except by explicit founder `/reset`.

## 8. When in doubt

Read, in order: CLAUDE.md → AGENTS.md → docs/rules/PROGRAMMING-RULES.md →
docs/rules/TOOL-STANDARDS.md → JARVIS-ARCHITECTURE.md. If the answer isn't
there, ask the founder — do not invent a new pattern. Simplicity beats
cleverness; if a simpler approach exists, propose it before building.
